From 62fc460f53584da51fe59c519c5c171c4fe3a482 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Sotraidis <56282582+chrissotraidis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 09:07:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] aurora: stretch the audio in time while the guest runs slow

When the game produces its 32 kHz mix slower than real time, the device drains
the queue and plays silence between pushes: in the iPad simulator's heavy
Outset view 84,072 of 213,206 pushes found less than 10 ms queued. The output
now goes through a synchronous overlap-add stretcher (audio_stretch.hpp:
40 ms sequences, 8 ms crossfades at the best-matching point within 15 ms),
which lengthens the sound without changing its pitch, so a slowdown sounds
slower rather than choppy. The factor aims the queue at 100 ms: 1 (bit-exact
pass-through) at or above it, up to 2 as it empties, smoothed over about
100 ms; nothing is stretched before playback starts. DOL_AUDIO_STRETCH=0 turns
it off. Standalone test: pass-through bit-exact; 1.1/1.3/1.6 give 1.088/1.288/
1.584 times the input with no step larger than the source's own; leaving the
stretcher is continuous. The shutdown line reports pushes, starved pushes,
stretched pushes and throttles. The acceptance capture is taken upstream, in
the host, and is unaffected.
